<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.4.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01294</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.4.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>f4ff59df-5c6d-47c5-b990-2586029bf5a8</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> Which expression is equal to \(4^0\cdot 4^2\)? </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> 0 </item>
  <item> 1 </item>
  <item> 16 </item>
  <item> 64 </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 16</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item><emphasis>Select three </emphasis> expressions that are equivalent to \(3^8\).</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(8^3\) </item>
  <item> \(\frac{3^{10}}{3^2}\) </item>
  <item> \(3\cdot 8\) </item>
  <item> \(\left(3^4\right)^2\) </item>
  <item> \(\left(3\cdot3\right)^4\) </item>
  <item> \(\frac1{3^8}\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(\frac{3^{10}}{3^2}\), \(\left(3^4\right)^2\) &amp; \(\left(3\cdot3\right)^4\)</para>

  

<para>For qustions 3-6 use the following table:</para>
<blockquote>
  <para>An investment bond is initially bought for $250. It doubles in value every decade. For each of the missing spaces in the table, a–d, select the missing value that helps complete the table.</para>
    
    <table class="os-raise-wideequaltable">
      <thead>
        <row>
          <entry scope="col" class="emphasis"> <para>decades since bond is bought</para>
          </entry>
          <entry scope="col" class="emphasis"> <para>dollar value of bond</para>
          </entry>
        </row>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <row>
          <entry><para>0</para></entry>
          <entry><para>3. _____</para></entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry><para>1</para></entry>
          <entry><para>4. _____</para></entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry><para>2</para></entry>
          <entry><para>5. _____</para></entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry><para>3</para></entry>
          <entry><para>6. _____</para></entry>
        </row>
      </tbody>
    </table>

</blockquote>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> The dollar value of the bond, 0 decades <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> $250 </item> 
  <item> The dollar value of the bond, 1 decade <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> $500 </item> 
  <item> The dollar value of the bond, 2 decades <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> $1000 </item> 
  <item> The dollar value of the bond, 3 decades <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> $2000  </item> 
</list>


<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>An investment bond is initially bought for $250. It doubles in value every decade. How many decades does it take before the bond is worth more than $10,000? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 6</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item> An investment bond is initially bought for $250. It doubles in value every decade. Choose an equation that relates \(V\)<emphasis>,</emphasis> the value of the bond, to \(d\), the number of decades since the bond was bought.</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(V = 250\cdot d^2\) </item>
  <item>\(V = 500^d\) </item>
  <item>\(V = 250\cdot 2\cdot d\) </item>
  <item>\(V = 250\cdot 2^d\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(V = 250\cdot 2^d\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
  <item>A sea turtle population \(p\) is modeled by the equation \(p=400\cdot(1.25)^y\) where \(y\) is the number of years since the population was first measured. How many turtles are in the population when it is first measured? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 400</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="10" type="enumerated">
  <item> A sea turtle population \(p\) is modeled by the equation \(p=400\cdot(1.25)^y\) where \(y\) is the number of years since the population was first measured. After how many years will the turtle population reach 700? Round to the nearest full year. <emphasis> </emphasis> </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 3</para>

<para>Use the following information to answer questions 11 and 12.</para>
<blockquote>
   <item>Bank Account A starts with $5,000 and grows by $1,000 each week. Bank Account B starts with $1 and doubles each week.<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated"/>
</item></blockquote> 
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="11" type="enumerated">
  <item> Which account has more money after one week? </item>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Bank Account A </para>

  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="12" type="enumerated">
    <item> Which account has more money after fifteen weeks?</item>
  </list>

<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Bank Account B </para>
    <para>For questions 13 - 15, use this graph showing the two account balances.</para>
      
      <blockquote>
      <para><figure><media alt="Graph of 2 discrete lines, origin O, with grid. Number of weeks and amount in dollars."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8a449ca635a6ee10b3f30217c5fe42a556930ad1"/></media></figure></para>
    </blockquote>
   <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="13" type="enumerated">
    <item> The graph of the red triangles corresponds to which bank account?</item>
  </list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Bank Account A </para>

  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="14" type="enumerated">
    <item> The graph of the blue circles corresponds to which bank account?</item>
  </list>

<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Bank Account B </para>

  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="15" type="enumerated">
    <item> Which of the two accounts would you choose for long-term saving?</item>
  </list>

<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Bank Account B </para>
</section>
